<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vortigern</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">5th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Gwrtheyrn</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vortigern was a 5th-century British ruler who, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gildas">Gildas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/historia-brittonum">Historia Brittonum</a>, invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hengist-and-horsa">Hengist and Horsa</a> to defend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, only for them to rebel and seize land. His historicity and dates are debated. Welsh genealogies made him the ancestor of the rulers of Gwrtheyrnion, whose name means 'the land of Gwrtheyrn', the Welsh form of his name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vortigern.jpg" alt="Vortigern" width="320" height="474" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Harvey · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vortigern_and_Rowena.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
